    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi prof, i am having a bit of trouble with phonetics. can u explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? like why is /z/ different from /s/ when they are made in the same place?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hello! Alveolar fricatives, like /s/ and /z/, are indeed made in the same spot—right behind your upper teeth on the alveolar ridge. The difference between them is *vibration*, or voicing, which is what makes them distinct. For the unvoiced /s/, your vocal cords are relaxed, so no vibration occurs as the air rushes through the narrow gap. /z/, on the other hand, is voiced, meaning your vocal cords are vibrating as the air passes through.",
